Sets the scale of the [`Image`](../struct.Image.html "struct iced::widget::Image").

The region of the [`Image`](../struct.Image.html "struct iced::widget::Image") drawn will be scaled from the center by the given scale factor. This can be useful to create certain effects and animations, like smooth zoom in / out.

[Source](https://docs.rs/iced_widget/0.14.0/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/src/iced_widget/image.rs.html#166)

#### pub fn [crop](#method.crop)(self, region: [Rectangle](../../struct.Rectangle.html "struct iced::Rectangle")<[u32](https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/primitive.u32.html)\>) -> [Image](../struct.Image.html "struct iced::widget::Image")<Handle>

Crops the [`Image`](../struct.Image.html "struct iced::widget::Image") to the given region described by the [`Rectangle`](../../struct.Rectangle.html "struct iced::Rectangle") in absolute coordinates.

Cropping is done before applying any transformation or [`ContentFit`](../../enum.ContentFit.html "enum iced::ContentFit"). In practice, this means that cropping an [`Image`](../struct.Image.html "struct iced::widget::Image") with this method should produce the same result as cropping it externally (e.g. with an image editor) and creating a new [`Handle`](../../advanced/image/enum.Handle.html "enum iced::advanced::image::Handle") for the cropped version.

However, this method is much more efficient; since it just leverages scissoring during rendering and no image cropping actually takes place. Instead, it reuses the existing image allocations and should be as efficient as not cropping at all!

The `region` coordinates will be clamped to the image dimensions, if necessary.

[Source](https://docs.rs/iced_widget/0.14.0/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/src/iced_widget/image.rs.html#175-178)

